WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.120
How can a resting cell switch on a gene in minutes, even with every ribosome

2
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.880
stopped? Add a growth factor, and c-fos transcripts still surge.

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.120
Whatever switched the gene on was already built and waiting.

4
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.160
The signal only had to flip it.

5
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:24.063
A transcription factor binds a short DNA site near a gene.

6
00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:29.523
Making a new factor is slow, since it must be transcribed and translated.

7
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:33.483
Fast pathways modify a factor already made.

8
00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:36.343
One waiting factor is CREB.

9
00:00:37.423 --> 00:00:42.063
It sits on DNA as a pair before any signal arrives, yet stays quiet.

10
00:00:42.063 --> 00:00:42.963
A transcription factor is slow, since it must be transcribed and translated.

11
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:47.887
When cyclic AMP rises, it frees the active subunits of PKA.

12
00:00:49.287 --> 00:00:54.327
Some enter the nucleus and attach one phosphate onto CREB at serine 133.

13
00:00:55.907 --> 00:00:57.847
That phosphate is a docking handle.

14
00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:04.847
CBP, a co-activator that cannot bind DNA itself, grips it through its KIX pocket.

15
00:01:05.987 --> 00:01:12.567
CBP then adds acetyl groups that loosen packaged DNA and calls in RNA polymerase.

16
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.987
Next, a different messenger: calcium.

17
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:22.887
Calcium binds calmodulin, and the pair switches on a nuclear kinase, CaMKIV.

18
00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:27.347
It puts a phosphate onto the exact same serine of CREB.

19
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:33.667
Growth factors reach that serine too, through kinases that ERK switches on.

20
00:01:34.887 --> 00:01:36.807
So separate signals flip one switch.

21
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.930
Adding a phosphate is one switch.

22
00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.610
Meet a second pre-made factor, NFAT, resting in the cytoplasm.

23
00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:51.130
It carries about a dozen phosphates that cover its nuclear entry tag.

24
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.070
Here, removing phosphates activates the factor, because those phosphates were

25
00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.070
locking it outside.

26
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:04.613
When the T cell receptor fires, sustained calcium turns on calcineurin, a

27
00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:06.733
phosphatase that removes phosphates.

28
00:02:07.853 --> 00:02:12.413
Pause and predict: once those phosphates come off, where does this factor go?

29
00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:23.437
The factor moves into the nucleus, because calcineurin strips the phosphates that

30
00:02:23.437 --> 00:02:24.637
covered its entry tag.

31
00:02:25.857 --> 00:02:30.197
With the tag exposed, it glides through a pore and switches on target genes.

32
00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:35.937
When calcium drops, kinases restore those phosphates and send it back out.

33
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:43.910
Two transplant drugs, cyclosporin A and FK506, quiet T cells by jamming this switch.

34
00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.710
Neither drug blocks calcineurin on its own.

35
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.850
Each binds a small helper protein.

36
00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.910
That pair sits beside calcineurin's active site and keeps protein substrates from

37
00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:57.910
reaching it.

38
00:02:58.483 --> 00:03:00.043
Back to the opening puzzle.

39
00:03:01.403 --> 00:03:06.083
Whether a signal adds one phosphate or removes many, the protein was built in

40
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:12.903
advance. Fast signals flip pre-made factors to switch on first-wave genes like c-fos

41
00:03:12.903 --> 00:03:17.463
within minutes. Stopped ribosomes cannot stop a waiting switch.
